offering services every day to thousands  and even millions of people is an  amazing challenge from a scale  perspective more specifically take  workforce development programs run by  cities and states they are extremely  important and as they support so many  people with an array of services that  range from job matching opportunities  personal development via job coaching  self-service resources to just name a  few  and i have learned a lot about how  running such meaningful programs  requires extensive coordination across  applicants employers community partners  and sharing knowledge broadly  2020 brought many challenges especially  as in-person operations were shut down  and many were left vulnerable to  unemployment the state of rhode island  responded to these challenges by  modernizing their workforce development  operations and moving completely to an  online platform called the virtual  career center also nicknamed vcc  it was developed in partnership with  google cloud and a non-profit called  research improving people's lives and  the vcc was fully built by google cloud  partner mavenwave as a quick intro  mavenwave helps a wide array of  organizations including public sector  customers with many types of cloud  initiatives whether it's big data and  machine learning building mobile  applications helping design positive  online user experiences moving  infrastructure to the cloud or deploying  collaboration tools like email chat  documents they even offer strategy and  technology advice today i've invited  joel osmond from mavenwave to share more  about that journey howdy my friend mind  sharing a bit about what you do so i  lead the maven wave digital experience  design our ux and design team as well as  our custom application development  practice and i am also the executive  sponsor for our virtual career center  solution that we've been bringing to  market with google  joel for the folks watching could you  briefly share what was the specific use  case your team focused on so the primary  use case that we looked at is how can we  apply  leading-edge emerging technologies to  help people get back to work and to use  them those tools in such a way that we  can augment the personal one-to-one  interactions that these agencies have  been using with job seekers to help them  get back to work that's beautiful joel  can you touch upon some of the products  it took to build the virtual career  center the vcc it comprises a number of  different google technologies um  obviously the custom pieces that we're  building for scheduling for  login authentication for data pipelines  and data analytics are all in google  cloud platform and we're doing custom  builds there that integrate into the  state's existing systems so we  essentially have taken things like  google me  google calendar google docs a lot of  those core tool sets that people use in  their everyday lives we've wrapped them  in this specific use case around the  virtual career center so when a job  coach is is going online to to update  their availability of when they can held  sessions that's really happening in the  background in google calendar and when a  job seeker and a job coach get together  in a video session it's actually  happening in google meet so those are  really the the engines of technologies  if you will that are driving the entire  vcc experience now if you don't mind  let's do a quick walkthrough of what  i've learned about your architecture and  then i would like to talk a bit more  about some of the design decisions  behind all of this for starters end  users mainly interact via an app built  on angular as the front end and is  connected to backend services hosted on  google kubernetes engine or relevant  google workspace products such as chat  calendar meet drive and docs  from a user journey perspective there  are three main types of users at this  time and that's job coaches job seekers  and employers  job coaches all have google ids in the  google workspace domain and therefore  authenticate against the google identity  repository job seekers are authenticated  through a cognito-based process  maintained by the nonprofit i mentioned  earlier with the rhode island  infrastructure team and the department  of information technology for contacts  prior to the start of the vcc project  the state of rhode island was making use  of this cognito identity repository to  authenticate users going through the  unemployment insurance application  process and rather than adding an  additional identity repository for users  interacting with the state the decision  was made to leverage the existing  cognito repository and its methods of  authentication in this release of the  virtual career center employers  participate directly with google meet  and to an extent google calendar but not  the annular app  to touch upon some of the google cloud  components specifically let's start with  firestore which is a real-time database  that keeps data in sync across client  apps it is used as the primary  persistence tool for the web pages it  also has a small role to play with the  login process for job coaches  next is bigquery which is a serverless  warehouse for data which is why it is  used primarily for reporting purposes  data studio is used to build filterable  dashboards over it  then there are cloud functions which are  functions you can trigger via snippets  of code and are used for a few purposes  in the solution such as populating jobs  and training data in the production  instance ensuring consistency between  google calendar and availability data  for job coaches and for populating  bigquery on a scheduled basis  then there's the kubernetes cluster on  which server side code runs in a single  region deployment with a minimum of four  nodes per zone across three zones of the  u.s east region the cluster is set to  auto scale in order to ensure that there  is sufficient processing power in the  event that there are spikes in activity  on the site  from a security standpoint there's cloud  armor which protects applications and  websites from attacks and in this  specific implementation there are  several sets of nist compliant policies  such as geofencing which prohibits  access to the site from outside of the  us  content is accessed through google's  content distribution network taking  advantage of caching wherever possible  and finally to manage the cycle of the  infrastructure a terraform script is  used which is an open source tool and is  structured into five folder environments  can you share a bit more about what was  the experience like for users before and  after the virtual career center was  launched sure so  in a sense the solution that we built it  changes the way job seekers interact  with the state in the state agency i  mean before the virtual career center  you know interested people there there  was a website and there were website  resources that people would utilize but  it was very much in many states uh you  know a hodgepodge of things that the  training opportunities there was often a  state database of jobs that they could  search into and it was some very basic  online capabilities but a lot of things  happen physically in the career center  you know there's a lot of that  face-to-face interaction where you know  coaches could really have an impact and  help job seekers  and the challenge when we built the  virtual career center is how can we keep  that intimacy how can we keep that  collaborative nature and how can we  dial up the online tools in a sense  because in many respects we were forced  to but try to keep that that tight  interaction we strive to keep that  personal interaction and like to say now  a job coach can talk to a job seeker on  opposite ends of the state and they can  really focus on getting the right advice  the right coach in front of the right  job seeker it's exciting to me to see  how we can continue to recreate these  safe spaces in a virtual format and on  top of that create better alignment  between job coaches and job seekers who  have expertise in navigating different  services this is thanks to having more  robust ways for job seekers to find  coaches with specific backgrounds such  as coaches who have a history with  helping college students and joel is  there a fun fact  you could share with us about the  project well i can share one interesting  fact we  before we launched the solution with  rhode island which was the initial  virtual career center uh state that we  we uh we deployed him we were a little  concerned about the adoption from the  career coaches you know they were  already having to deal with working from  home  we showed them what it was going to be  like  we we tried to everything we could to  ease them into it and we did it with  sort of holding our breath you know  what's going to come of this  uh we were actually pleasantly surprised  they were absolutely uh excited for the  solution they were commenting how it's  really going to help them out they  couldn't wait they were asking how soon  they can get it so it was kind of  interesting i think we were overly not  pessimistic we're more concerned of what  the reactions would be but they just  embraced it wholeheartedly and were very  excited it got us very excited that uh  you know we are doing the right thing  we've created the right experience and  just seeing them absolutely pounce on  the solution was it was very gratifying  yeah i think that fear of whether a new  tool is going to be well received or not  especially amongst so much change in the  environment is extremely relatable to it  practitioners and so your approach is an  excellent example of how important it is  to scope time to collect user feedback  and to do it very early on and as often  as possible when designing systems  another topic i want to touch upon is  data by having more data centralized in  a digital format there are greater  opportunities around analytics and  insights what were some design  considerations with data working a lot  with data in the virtual career center  solution in particular we're looking at  analytics right if we're going to  implement a solution like this key  stakeholders in the state right up to  the governor's offices they want to know  is it working are people finding jobs  are people having career sessions so  there's a lot of analytics to track  what's working and not working and we  can do this in a virtualized solution  and we talk a lot about creating  analytics and dashboards not just for  stakeholders within the state who are  interested in how the virtual career  center is doing but we talk a lot about  employers because a lot of times  employers are posting jobs and want to  use those virtual career centers to  connect to job seekers so we're talking  about an employer dashboard so they can  understand who's out there who who's the  positive candidate pool and how can they  see how the job center uh the virtual  career center can benefit them and we  even talk about personal dashboards do  you envision any technical enhancements  in the future to this architecture and  is this solution only for state and  local entities so the virtual career  center or the vcc as we call it it's  actually comprised of three pillars this  way we talk about it one is around  modernizing digital experiences and this  is taking what is for many states a an  older design older set of technologies  that they used for very basic online  functionality and giving that all a new  modern look a modern face a central  place for people to go for all their  needs and requirements as a job seeker  within the state so that's the  modernizing the second pillar is around  new innovations can we bring machine  learning and artificial intelligence to  job searches can we help with resume  building or resume parsing where i can  actually take keywords out of a resume  and match those to certain jobs  more of in a clustering machine learning  model rather than just a raw keyword  search we also looked at chat bots and  the ability to take some of the load off  the call centers and states and  utilizing natural language processing to  help guide job seekers and answer  questions so and we looked at a number  of those types of innovations the third  pillar in the virtual career center is  around taking what was a brick and  mortar physical career center and  putting that online and doing it  virtually using google meet and other  technologies to create a virtual version  of what was happening in those physical  career centers so far every state we've  talked to they tend to choose different  things it's interesting because you  think they all need the same  you know have the same needs but  their own history their technology  uh savviness the needs of their  constituents it tends to vary and so we  find different commissioner stakeholders  different states  you know they need different pieces and  that's why it's nice to be able to put  out a we almost call it the menu we tell  states you can order off the menu and  depending what you order you know  obviously they have prices just like a  menu and so that's how we work through  budgets and solutions with them and it's  been interesting to see different states  so beyond just state governments we've  actually had some interesting  opportunities to talk about the virtual  career center and the technologies and  the solution with some counties with  some other uh  other countries and a number of  universities you know everywhere you've  got people looking for jobs or people  looking for additional training or  looking to you know better their careers  there's an opportunity in the virtual  career center well thank you joel for  walking us through so much great  information about your journey today  to wrap up and leave on a positive note  what was a memorable moment you  experienced  one of the things that i have said is  you know throughout the pandemic i think  for me personally and i think for a lot  of the team  uh  you know we're not front line workers  we're not health care workers we're not  grocery store workers and you know we  were kind fell along for the ride in  some respects during this whole thing  and so being able to be part of a  solution that is doing our part i mean  we may be on the i.t side of it but just  being able to  you know do something in our field to  help people's lives get better to help  people find jobs coming out of the  pandemic get a job that maybe they lost  to the pandemic it really felt like you  know doing our part versus being on the  sidelines through this whole crisis  ah yes this work is quite meaningful and  i think it's an epic milestone to be  able to scale operations to a virtual  format in this way which speaks to the  beauty of building in the cloud and  designing for a positive user experience  well friends i left several helpful  links in this video's description in  case you want to check them out if you  like this type of content then feel free  to watch more episodes on architecting  in google cloud or subscribe to the  channel  cheers  [Music]  you

